./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:1:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 1, "text": "Institute of Health Informatics University College London yunsoo.kim.23@ucl.ac.uk", "contextualized_text": "Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs: Integrating Knowledge Graphs and Protein Structures to Large Language Models\nYunsoo Kim\nInstitute of Health Informatics University College London yunsoo.kim.23@ucl.ac.uk", "headings": ["Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs: Integrating Knowledge Graphs and Protein Structures to Large Language Models", "Yunsoo Kim"], "pages": [1], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/2", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 1, "bbox": {"l": 158.49992525, "t": 694.5033437786775, "r": 436.78307391176, "b": 667.3913130449175, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 81]}]}], "headings": ["Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs: Integrating Knowledge Graphs and Protein Structures to Large Language Models", "Yunsoo Kim"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:2:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 2, "text": "Transformer model has been a de-facto standard in natural language processing. Its adaptations in other fields such as computer vision showed promising results that this architecture is a powerful neural network in representation learning regardless of the data type. This recent success has led to research in multimodal Large Language Model (LLM), which enabled us to new types of tasks and applications with multiple data types. However, multimodal LLM in the biomedical domain is primarily limited to images, text, and/or sequence data. Here I propose to work on multimodal LLM architecture for biomedical graphs such as protein structure and chemical molecules. The research hypothesis is based on the fact that clinicians and researchers in computational biology and clinical research take advantage of various information for their decision-making process. Therefore, an AI model being able to handle multiple data types should boost its ability to use diverse knowledge for improved performances in clinical applications.", "contextualized_text": "Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs: Integrating Knowledge Graphs and Protein Structures to Large Language Models\nAbstract\nTransformer model has been a de-facto standard in natural language processing. Its adaptations in other fields such as computer vision showed promising results that this architecture is a powerful neural network in representation learning regardless of the data type. This recent success has led to research in multimodal Large Language Model (LLM), which enabled us to new types of tasks and applications with multiple data types. However, multimodal LLM in the biomedical domain is primarily limited to images, text, and/or sequence data. Here I propose to work on multimodal LLM architecture for biomedical graphs such as protein structure and chemical molecules. The research hypothesis is based on the fact that clinicians and researchers in computational biology and clinical research take advantage of various information for their decision-making process. Therefore, an AI model being able to handle multiple data types should boost its ability to use diverse knowledge for improved performances in clinical applications.", "headings": ["Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs: Integrating Knowledge Graphs and Protein Structures to Large Language Models", "Abstract"], "pages": [1], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/4", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 1, "bbox": {"l": 87.8653937, "t": 600.4224775705576, "r": 273.7818859858463, "b": 325.3897936239075, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 1029]}]}], "headings": ["Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs: Integrating Knowledge Graphs and Protein Structures to Large Language Models", "Abstract"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:3:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 3, "text": "The foundation model revolutionized not only natural language processing (NLP) but also the humanAI interaction after the release of ChatGPT service by OpenAI (OpenAI, 2023a). ChatGPT enhanced the usability with a chat interface allowing users to instruct large language model (LLM) for any tasks even the ones requiring complex domain knowledge such as medical domain text (Savage et al., 2024). The emergence of open-source medical LLMs has further enhanced access to these technologies in healthcare settings, addressing privacy concerns associated with patient data (Toma et al., 2023; Kweon et al., 2023; Chen et al., 2023).\nThis success of the foundation model quickly extended to computer vision (CV), expanding the application of chat assistant tools to medical image analytics (OpenAI, 2023b; Li et al., 2023b;", "contextualized_text": "Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs: Integrating Knowledge Graphs and Protein Structures to Large Language Models\n1 Introduction\nThe foundation model revolutionized not only natural language processing (NLP) but also the humanAI interaction after the release of ChatGPT service by OpenAI (OpenAI, 2023a). ChatGPT enhanced the usability with a chat interface allowing users to instruct large language model (LLM) for any tasks even the ones requiring complex domain knowledge such as medical domain text (Savage et al., 2024). The emergence of open-source medical LLMs has further enhanced access to these technologies in healthcare settings, addressing privacy concerns associated with patient data (Toma et al., 2023; Kweon et al., 2023; Chen et al., 2023).\nThis success of the foundation model quickly extended to computer vision (CV), expanding the application of chat assistant tools to medical image analytics (OpenAI, 2023b; Li et al., 2023b;", "headings": ["Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs: Integrating Knowledge Graphs and Protein Structures to Large Language Models", "1 Introduction"], "pages": [1], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/6", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 1, "bbox": {"l": 70.46352365, "t": 295.91981919885757, "r": 290.9474252390972, "b": 120.17456687558251, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 629]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/7", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 1, "bbox": {"l": 70.8565433, "t": 119.77101219885753, "r": 290.9429304651698, "b": 65.97585707558255, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 189]}]}], "headings": ["Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs: Integrating Knowledge Graphs and Protein Structures to Large Language Models", "1 Introduction"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:4:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 4, "text": "Tu et al., 2023). Recently, visual instruction tuning was introduced to open the possibility of a visual assistant in medicine (Li et al., 2023a; Lee et al., 2023). Additionally, there has been notable progress in extending the model's capabilities to handle biological sequences, including DNA sequences and chemical sequences represented by Simplified Molecular Input Line Entry Specification (SMILES) notation (Taylor et al., 2022; Consens et al., 2023; Zhang et al., 2024).\nDespite these advancements, multimodal research in biomedicine has focused on integrating text, image, and sequence data. While these modalities have proven invaluable in capturing certain medical nuances, they often overlook the structural intricacies inherent in biomedical graph data, such as knowledge graphs and protein structures. Consequently, the full potential of multimodal learning remains largely unexplored in addressing the multifaceted challenges encountered in computational biology and clinical research.", "contextualized_text": "Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs: Integrating Knowledge Graphs and Protein Structures to Large Language Models\n1 Introduction\nTu et al., 2023). Recently, visual instruction tuning was introduced to open the possibility of a visual assistant in medicine (Li et al., 2023a; Lee et al., 2023). Additionally, there has been notable progress in extending the model's capabilities to handle biological sequences, including DNA sequences and chemical sequences represented by Simplified Molecular Input Line Entry Specification (SMILES) notation (Taylor et al., 2022; Consens et al., 2023; Zhang et al., 2024).\nDespite these advancements, multimodal research in biomedicine has focused on integrating text, image, and sequence data. While these modalities have proven invaluable in capturing certain medical nuances, they often overlook the structural intricacies inherent in biomedical graph data, such as knowledge graphs and protein structures. Consequently, the full potential of multimodal learning remains largely unexplored in addressing the multifaceted challenges encountered in computational biology and clinical research.", "headings": ["Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs: Integrating Knowledge Graphs and Protein Structures to Large Language Models", "1 Introduction"], "pages": [1], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/8", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 1, "bbox": {"l": 305.80629020000003, "t": 621.1170782488575, "r": 526.2378946311701, "b": 486.0218583255826, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 477]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/9", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 1, "bbox": {"l": 306.14430710000005, "t": 485.4962975488576, "r": 526.238549209898, "b": 336.85140017558257, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 521]}]}], "headings": ["Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs: Integrating Knowledge Graphs and Protein Structures to Large Language Models", "1 Introduction"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:5:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 5, "text": "Graph-based representations in biology and medicine are effective in elucidating the complex mechanisms of diseases and uncovering novel insights, such as biomarkers and therapeutic targets (Zhang et al., 2021; Chandak et al., 2023). Over the years, there has been a notable shift in graph representation learning methodologies, moving from traditional graph neural networks to transformer model architectures, mirroring advancements seen in other modalities. Notably, transformer models have shown considerable promise in graph representation learning, particularly for small biomedical graphs like chemical molecules. This approach has demonstrated the ability to overcome challenges such as over-smoothing observed in graph neural networks, while also exhibiting improved performance with deeper models (Ying et al., 2021).\nFigure 1: Overview of Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs", "contextualized_text": "1.1 Biomedical Graphs\nGraph-based representations in biology and medicine are effective in elucidating the complex mechanisms of diseases and uncovering novel insights, such as biomarkers and therapeutic targets (Zhang et al., 2021; Chandak et al., 2023). Over the years, there has been a notable shift in graph representation learning methodologies, moving from traditional graph neural networks to transformer model architectures, mirroring advancements seen in other modalities. Notably, transformer models have shown considerable promise in graph representation learning, particularly for small biomedical graphs like chemical molecules. This approach has demonstrated the ability to overcome challenges such as over-smoothing observed in graph neural networks, while also exhibiting improved performance with deeper models (Ying et al., 2021).\nFigure 1: Overview of Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs", "headings": ["1.1 Biomedical Graphs"], "pages": [1, 2], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/11", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 1, "bbox": {"l": 305.80629020000003, "t": 309.46949664885756, "r": 526.3338122340106, "b": 65.97585707558255, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 826]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/14", "parent": {"cref": "#/pictures/0"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "caption", "prov": [{"page_no": 2, "bbox": {"l": 168.11040575, "t": 521.6175375205576, "r": 427.17088332625974, "b": 509.61200427390753, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 60]}]}], "headings": ["1.1 Biomedical Graphs"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:6:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 6, "text": "As clinicians and researchers rely on multimodal data to make their decisions regarding patient care, there exists a pressing need to extend the scope of biomedical multimodal models to cover various modalities such as biomedical graphs (Soman et al., 2023; Lv et al., 2024). This extension holds the promise of significantly enhancing the capabilities of foundation models in biomedical research, thereby broadening the horizons for a myriad of biomedical tasks, including drug discovery, differential diagnoses, and treatment planning.\nIn light of these considerations, the proposed research aims to bridge the gap between foundation models and biomedical graph data, leveraging the rich structural information encoded in graphs to enhance the capabilities of multimodal learning in biomedical research. The overarching objective is to develop novel methodologies and frameworks that effectively harness the synergies between foundation models and biomedical graph data, enabling clinicians and researchers to derive deeper insights from complex biological networks.\nFigure 1 shows how different modalities including the biomedical graphs such as protein and knowledge graph will be fused with the medical foundation model. With this foundation model, clinicians and researchers can use prompts with their expertise for clinical and biomedical applications for precision medicine and drug discovery.", "contextualized_text": "1.2 Thesis Objective\nAs clinicians and researchers rely on multimodal data to make their decisions regarding patient care, there exists a pressing need to extend the scope of biomedical multimodal models to cover various modalities such as biomedical graphs (Soman et al., 2023; Lv et al., 2024). This extension holds the promise of significantly enhancing the capabilities of foundation models in biomedical research, thereby broadening the horizons for a myriad of biomedical tasks, including drug discovery, differential diagnoses, and treatment planning.\nIn light of these considerations, the proposed research aims to bridge the gap between foundation models and biomedical graph data, leveraging the rich structural information encoded in graphs to enhance the capabilities of multimodal learning in biomedical research. The overarching objective is to develop novel methodologies and frameworks that effectively harness the synergies between foundation models and biomedical graph data, enabling clinicians and researchers to derive deeper insights from complex biological networks.\nFigure 1 shows how different modalities including the biomedical graphs such as protein and knowledge graph will be fused with the medical foundation model. With this foundation model, clinicians and researchers can use prompts with their expertise for clinical and biomedical applications for precision medicine and drug discovery.", "headings": ["1.2 Thesis Objective"], "pages": [2], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/16", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 2, "bbox": {"l": 70.46352365, "t": 464.5522503988575, "r": 290.951832735861, "b": 315.90735302558255, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 537]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/17", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 2, "bbox": {"l": 70.8565433, "t": 312.48664749885756, "r": 290.95078540989755, "b": 163.8417501255825, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 530]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/18", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 2, "bbox": {"l": 70.8565433, "t": 160.42104459885752, "r": 291.04903767686517, "b": 65.97585707558244, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 332]}]}], "headings": ["1.2 Thesis Objective"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:7:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 7, "text": "For instance, the model can be queried to find a disease that can be cured with an existing drug.\nThe thesis proposes to explore the hypothesis that multimodal representation learning with biomedical graphs will improve the performance of drug discovery and precision medicine applications of foundation models. To achieve this objective, the research aims to:\n1. Develop a novel state-of-the-art foundation model with genetics and pharmacology related biomedical guidelines to better understand human diseases.\n2. Extend the modality of the developed LLM to interpret biomedical graphs as well as other modalities in the biomedical domain.\n3. Compare the performance of the multimodal model with unimodal models and other stateof-the-art methods.\n4. Use the foundation model for applications such as target identification and drug repurposing especially for neurodegenerative diseases such as Dementia.\nThrough these objectives, the research aims to contribute towards advancing precision medicine and healthcare innovation, paving the way for personalized and targeted approaches to healthcare using the foundation model. So far, I am working on the first objective, training a biomedical LLM and the preliminary result for this objective is included in this proposal.", "contextualized_text": "1.2 Thesis Objective\nFor instance, the model can be queried to find a disease that can be cured with an existing drug.\nThe thesis proposes to explore the hypothesis that multimodal representation learning with biomedical graphs will improve the performance of drug discovery and precision medicine applications of foundation models. To achieve this objective, the research aims to:\n1. Develop a novel state-of-the-art foundation model with genetics and pharmacology related biomedical guidelines to better understand human diseases.\n2. Extend the modality of the developed LLM to interpret biomedical graphs as well as other modalities in the biomedical domain.\n3. Compare the performance of the multimodal model with unimodal models and other stateof-the-art methods.\n4. Use the foundation model for applications such as target identification and drug repurposing especially for neurodegenerative diseases such as Dementia.\nThrough these objectives, the research aims to contribute towards advancing precision medicine and healthcare innovation, paving the way for personalized and targeted approaches to healthcare using the foundation model. So far, I am working on the first objective, training a biomedical LLM and the preliminary result for this objective is included in this proposal.", "headings": ["1.2 Thesis Objective"], "pages": [2], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/19", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 2, "bbox": {"l": 306.1443071, "t": 488.0194236988575, "r": 524.427984450186, "b": 461.32362347558256, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 97]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/20", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 2, "bbox": {"l": 306.1443071, "t": 455.6028029488575, "r": 526.234958118352, "b": 374.70729287558254, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 262]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/21", "parent": {"cref": "#/groups/0"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "list_item", "prov": [{"page_no": 2, "bbox": {"l": 314.79973985000004, "t": 368.98647234885755, "r": 526.239221971534, "b": 315.19031717558255, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 150]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/22", "parent": {"cref": "#/groups/0"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "list_item", "prov": [{"page_no": 2, "bbox": {"l": 314.79973985000004, "t": 314.78676249885757, "r": 524.6034297320111, "b": 274.5412848255826, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 128]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/23", "parent": {"cref": "#/groups/0"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "list_item", "prov": [{"page_no": 2, "bbox": {"l": 314.79973985000004, "t": 274.13673009885747, "r": 526.2287923504792, "b": 233.89125242558248, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 106]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/24", "parent": {"cref": "#/groups/0"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "list_item", "prov": [{"page_no": 2, "bbox": {"l": 314.79973985000004, "t": 233.4876977488575, "r": 526.2363854637159, "b": 179.6915425755825, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 155]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/25", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 2, "bbox": {"l": 305.80629020000003, "t": 173.9707220488575, "r": 526.2322870734063, "b": 65.97585707558244, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 366]}]}], "headings": ["1.2 Thesis Objective"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:8:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 8, "text": "The proposed foundation model for biomedical graphs, depicted in Figure 1, integrates multiple specialized encoders with a backbone LLM that is also trained for the biomedical domain to effectively process and understand diverse biomedical data types such as medical images and ontologies.", "contextualized_text": "1.2 Thesis Objective\n2 Methods\nThe proposed foundation model for biomedical graphs, depicted in Figure 1, integrates multiple specialized encoders with a backbone LLM that is also trained for the biomedical domain to effectively process and understand diverse biomedical data types such as medical images and ontologies.", "headings": ["1.2 Thesis Objective", "2 Methods"], "pages": [3], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/28", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 70.51852640000001, "t": 748.1454293488576, "r": 290.9422104285698, "b": 667.2499192755826, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 289]}]}], "headings": ["1.2 Thesis Objective", "2 Methods"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:9:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 9, "text": "The following open-source LLMs were used to investigate their performance in handling medical domain text.\nLLaMA2 (Touvron et al., 2023) . LLaMA2, 7B, 13B, and 70B models without chat optimization were used in this work. These models were trained on 2 trillion (T) pretraining tokens in the general domain. There are several medical LLMs further trained from LLaMA2 model weights (Toma et al., 2023; Kweon et al., 2023; Chen et al., 2023). Among these medical LLMs, the Meditron 70B model claims to be the best-performing model (Chen et al., 2023). The recent version of the LLaMA family model, LLaMA-3 (Meta, 2024) 8B, was also used in this work. The pretraining corpus was increased to 15 T tokens.", "contextualized_text": "2.1 Medical Language Encoder\nThe following open-source LLMs were used to investigate their performance in handling medical domain text.\nLLaMA2 (Touvron et al., 2023) . LLaMA2, 7B, 13B, and 70B models without chat optimization were used in this work. These models were trained on 2 trillion (T) pretraining tokens in the general domain. There are several medical LLMs further trained from LLaMA2 model weights (Toma et al., 2023; Kweon et al., 2023; Chen et al., 2023). Among these medical LLMs, the Meditron 70B model claims to be the best-performing model (Chen et al., 2023). The recent version of the LLaMA family model, LLaMA-3 (Meta, 2024) 8B, was also used in this work. The pretraining corpus was increased to 15 T tokens.", "headings": ["2.1 Medical Language Encoder"], "pages": [3], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/30", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 70.51852640000001, "t": 638.0989272988576, "r": 289.13603890110875, "b": 597.8534496255826, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 106]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/31", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 70.0385024, "t": 597.3956198352375, "r": 291.0439974206649, "b": 421.2586203255826, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 593]}]}], "headings": ["2.1 Medical Language Encoder"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:10:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 10, "text": "Mistral (Jiang et al., 2023) Mistral-7B-v0.1 without chat optimization was used. While the details of the training dataset remain undisclosed, Mistral is known to utilize Grouped Query Attention, similar to Llama2-70B, along with Sliding Window Attention. For the biomedical LLM, BioMistral is one of the first models in the biomedicine domain based on the Mistral model (Labrak et al., 2024).\nPhi-2 (Microsoft, 2023) Phi-2 model is the smallest model in this study. Phi-2 is 2.7B parameters and is trained on an augmented textbook corpus consisting of 1.4 T tokens. Other training details remain undisclosed. As far as my understanding, no Phi-2 model was trained for the biomedical domain at the time of conducting the research.", "contextualized_text": "2.1 Medical Language Encoder\nMistral (Jiang et al., 2023) Mistral-7B-v0.1 without chat optimization was used. While the details of the training dataset remain undisclosed, Mistral is known to utilize Grouped Query Attention, similar to Llama2-70B, along with Sliding Window Attention. For the biomedical LLM, BioMistral is one of the first models in the biomedicine domain based on the Mistral model (Labrak et al., 2024).\nPhi-2 (Microsoft, 2023) Phi-2 model is the smallest model in this study. Phi-2 is 2.7B parameters and is trained on an augmented textbook corpus consisting of 1.4 T tokens. Other training details remain undisclosed. As far as my understanding, no Phi-2 model was trained for the biomedical domain at the time of conducting the research.", "headings": ["2.1 Medical Language Encoder"], "pages": [3], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/32", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 70.46352365, "t": 420.8007905352376, "r": 290.94293046516987, "b": 298.86350087558253, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 393]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/33", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 70.8565433, "t": 298.4056710852376, "r": 290.95078540989766, "b": 203.56773632558247, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 336]}]}], "headings": ["2.1 Medical Language Encoder"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:11:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 11, "text": "Phi-3 (Abdin et al., 2024) Lastly, I selected the Phi-3 model, which is slightly larger (3.8B parameters) and a recent version of the Phi model. The training corpus became larger as well (3.3 T tokens). Just like the Phi-2 model, the Phi-3 model trained for the biomedical domain did not exist.", "contextualized_text": "2.1 Medical Language Encoder\nPhi-3 (Abdin et al., 2024) Lastly, I selected the Phi-3 model, which is slightly larger (3.8B parameters) and a recent version of the Phi model. The training corpus became larger as well (3.3 T tokens). Just like the Phi-2 model, the Phi-3 model trained for the biomedical domain did not exist.", "headings": ["2.1 Medical Language Encoder"], "pages": [3], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/34", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 70.8565433, "t": 203.10990653523754, "r": 290.9477307091701, "b": 121.82164922558252, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 294]}]}], "headings": ["2.1 Medical Language Encoder"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:12:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 12, "text": "SigLIP model trained at resolution 512X512 will be used for the vision encoder (Zhai et al., 2023).\nIt is a CLIP model with an improved loss, sigmoid loss. For medical image and text alignment training, the MIMIC-CXR dataset, which is made up of chest X-ray images and corresponding radiology reports, will be used (Johnson et al., 2019). Also, various types of clinical notes at University College London Hospitals will be used as well as national resources such as the Scottish Medical Imaging (SMI) archive (Baxter et al., 2023). It contains 54 million reports and medical images such as MRIs.", "contextualized_text": "2.2 Vision Encoder\nSigLIP model trained at resolution 512X512 will be used for the vision encoder (Zhai et al., 2023).\nIt is a CLIP model with an improved loss, sigmoid loss. For medical image and text alignment training, the MIMIC-CXR dataset, which is made up of chest X-ray images and corresponding radiology reports, will be used (Johnson et al., 2019). Also, various types of clinical notes at University College London Hospitals will be used as well as national resources such as the Scottish Medical Imaging (SMI) archive (Baxter et al., 2023). It contains 54 million reports and medical images such as MRIs.", "headings": ["2.2 Vision Encoder"], "pages": [3], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/36", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 70.8565433, "t": 92.67165729885755, "r": 291.04308101044677, "b": 65.97585707558255, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 99]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/37", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 305.7842891, "t": 770.1655302988576, "r": 525.7895082029701, "b": 635.0703103755825, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 496]}]}], "headings": ["2.2 Vision Encoder"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:13:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 13, "text": "For encoding biological sequences such as DNA sequences, protein sequences, and SMILES representations of chemical structures, I propose to use the Galactica mini and base models (Taylor et al., 2022). Galactica stands out as the only option specifically trained to handle a diverse range of biological sequence data types, for specialized embedding capturing the unique characteristics of DNA, protein, and chemical sequences.", "contextualized_text": "2.3 Sequence Encoder\nFor encoding biological sequences such as DNA sequences, protein sequences, and SMILES representations of chemical structures, I propose to use the Galactica mini and base models (Taylor et al., 2022). Galactica stands out as the only option specifically trained to handle a diverse range of biological sequence data types, for specialized embedding capturing the unique characteristics of DNA, protein, and chemical sequences.", "headings": ["2.3 Sequence Encoder"], "pages": [3], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/39", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 306.14430710000005, "t": 607.6124030488576, "r": 526.2306942651701, "b": 486.06686057558255, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 427]}]}], "headings": ["2.3 Sequence Encoder"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:14:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 14, "text": "Considering the absence of a single graph transformer model trained to handle knowledge graphs, protein structures, and chemical structures simultaneously, I plan to train a graph transformer model tailored for this purpose. However, one of the current limitations of existing graph transformer architectures lies in their constrained input size. To address this limitation, linear attention or any other efficient attention can enable the model to handle larger graphs effectively.\nThe training data for this encoder will be collected from previous works with protein structure and chemical molecule structure encoding (Hie et al., 2022; Ying et al., 2021). For the knowledge graph training dataset, I plan to construct the graph from biomedical entities recognized from clinical notes and biomedical papers. By leveraging these datasets, I aim to train a robust Graph Transformer model capable of effectively encoding diverse types of graph data.", "contextualized_text": "2.4 Graph Encoder\nConsidering the absence of a single graph transformer model trained to handle knowledge graphs, protein structures, and chemical structures simultaneously, I plan to train a graph transformer model tailored for this purpose. However, one of the current limitations of existing graph transformer architectures lies in their constrained input size. To address this limitation, linear attention or any other efficient attention can enable the model to handle larger graphs effectively.\nThe training data for this encoder will be collected from previous works with protein structure and chemical molecule structure encoding (Hie et al., 2022; Ying et al., 2021). For the knowledge graph training dataset, I plan to construct the graph from biomedical entities recognized from clinical notes and biomedical papers. By leveraging these datasets, I aim to train a robust Graph Transformer model capable of effectively encoding diverse types of graph data.", "headings": ["2.4 Graph Encoder"], "pages": [3], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/41", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 306.14430710000005, "t": 458.6089532488576, "r": 526.2380910047883, "b": 323.5137333255825, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 482]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/42", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 306.14430710000005, "t": 322.9741718488576, "r": 526.234958118352, "b": 187.87895192558256, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 465]}]}], "headings": ["2.4 Graph Encoder"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:15:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 15, "text": "Once the encoder for each modality is trained, alignment using multi-layer perceptron adapters between the medical LLM and encoders will be implemented, an approach inspired by LLaVA family models (Li et al., 2023a; Lee et al., 2023). This will enable the foundation model to comprehend various modalities. Training data will be constructed for this alignment, as well as for reinforcement learning to train the model for the expected output of various downstream tasks.", "contextualized_text": "2.5 Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs\nOnce the encoder for each modality is trained, alignment using multi-layer perceptron adapters between the medical LLM and encoders will be implemented, an approach inspired by LLaVA family models (Li et al., 2023a; Lee et al., 2023). This will enable the foundation model to comprehend various modalities. Training data will be constructed for this alignment, as well as for reinforcement learning to train the model for the expected output of various downstream tasks.", "headings": ["2.5 Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs"], "pages": [3, 4], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/44", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 3, "bbox": {"l": 306.14430710000005, "t": 160.42104459885752, "r": 526.238549209898, "b": 65.97585707558255, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 340]}, {"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 70.8565433, "t": 770.1655302988576, "r": 289.14022065018577, "b": 729.9190525755826, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [341, 470]}]}], "headings": ["2.5 Foundation Model for Biomedical Graphs"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:16:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 16, "text": "I aim to work on datasets for brain diseases such as dementia and multiple sclerosis.\nDementia is a syndrome caused by many diseases including Alzheimer's disease. It affects memory and cognition, and symptoms become worse over time without cures. The foundation model will be used for the diagnosis and prognosis of dementia, aiding in precision medicine. For the diagnosis, the memory test report as well as the genetic expression profile will be used to diagnose the patient. The model will be also used to estimate biomarkers for Alzheimer's disease prognosis such as brain volume from MRIs. The patient's speech language ability is also another important data that the model will interpret for the prognosis.", "contextualized_text": "2.6 Downstream tasks\nI aim to work on datasets for brain diseases such as dementia and multiple sclerosis.\nDementia is a syndrome caused by many diseases including Alzheimer's disease. It affects memory and cognition, and symptoms become worse over time without cures. The foundation model will be used for the diagnosis and prognosis of dementia, aiding in precision medicine. For the diagnosis, the memory test report as well as the genetic expression profile will be used to diagnose the patient. The model will be also used to estimate biomarkers for Alzheimer's disease prognosis such as brain volume from MRIs. The patient's speech language ability is also another important data that the model will interpret for the prognosis.", "headings": ["2.6 Downstream tasks"], "pages": [4], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/47", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 70.8565433, "t": 703.0051724488576, "r": 289.1332384770947, "b": 676.3083721755825, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 85]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/48", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 70.46352365, "t": 675.8688156988576, "r": 290.94719431835176, "b": 500.1245634255826, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 627]}]}], "headings": ["2.6 Downstream tasks"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:17:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 17, "text": "Multiple sclerosis is a brain disease that changes our immune system to attack the myelin sheath. It can cause disability but has no cure. I aim to work on target identification for drug discovery. For target identification tasks, I propose to analyze single-cell disease-gene association networks sourced from the SC2disease dataset (Zhao et al., 2021). This dataset contains comparisons of gene expressions of different multiple sclerosis diseaserelated health status. It can thereby provide valuable insights into disease-gene associations at the single-cell level, and offer rich data for comprehensive analysis and interpretation.", "contextualized_text": "2.6 Downstream tasks\nMultiple sclerosis is a brain disease that changes our immune system to attack the myelin sheath. It can cause disability but has no cure. I aim to work on target identification for drug discovery. For target identification tasks, I propose to analyze single-cell disease-gene association networks sourced from the SC2disease dataset (Zhao et al., 2021). This dataset contains comparisons of gene expressions of different multiple sclerosis diseaserelated health status. It can thereby provide valuable insights into disease-gene associations at the single-cell level, and offer rich data for comprehensive analysis and interpretation.", "headings": ["2.6 Downstream tasks"], "pages": [4], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/49", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 70.46352365, "t": 499.68400689885755, "r": 291.0430810104467, "b": 323.9397546255825, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 635]}]}], "headings": ["2.6 Downstream tasks"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:18:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 18, "text": "The training dataset was collected from MedlinePlus 1 which includes a medical encyclopedia and texts about drugs and genetics. The collected training dataset for continued pretraining was 2.2 million tokens based on Phi-2 . Continued pretraining was done for all the models with an epoch of 3 and a learning rate of 5e-5.", "contextualized_text": "3.1 Medical LLM Training\nThe training dataset was collected from MedlinePlus 1 which includes a medical encyclopedia and texts about drugs and genetics. The collected training dataset for continued pretraining was 2.2 million tokens based on Phi-2 . Continued pretraining was done for all the models with an epoch of 3 and a learning rate of 5e-5.", "headings": ["3.1 Medical LLM Training"], "pages": [4], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/52", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 70.46352365, "t": 274.6417553488575, "r": 290.94987655445703, "b": 180.1955677755825, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 322]}]}], "headings": ["3.1 Medical LLM Training"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:19:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 19, "text": "Figure 2 illustrates the breakdown of the MedlinePlus corpus categories. The largest category, Health Conditions, comprises 26.1% of the corpus and includes information on the frequency, causes, synonyms, and inheritance patterns of various diseases. The Genes category, accounting for 20.3% of the corpus, describes the normal functions of human genes and the health implications of genetic modifications. The Medical Tests category, making up 19.9% of the corpus, covers tests such as allergy skin tests, detailing their purposes, procedures, and possible results. The Health Topics category constitutes 18.8% of the corpus and serves as an encyclopedia covering body parts, therapies, and wellness issues, with content regularly reviewed and updated daily. The Introduction to Genetics category, comprising 9.75% of the dataset, provides fundamental explanations of human genetics concepts. Finally, the Dietary Supplements category, representing 5.15% of the dataset, offers descriptions of the effectiveness, usual dosages, and potential drug interactions of various supplements.\n1 https://medlineplus.gov/\nFigure 2: MedlinePlus Corpus Categories in tokens", "contextualized_text": "3.1 Medical LLM Training\nFigure 2 illustrates the breakdown of the MedlinePlus corpus categories. The largest category, Health Conditions, comprises 26.1% of the corpus and includes information on the frequency, causes, synonyms, and inheritance patterns of various diseases. The Genes category, accounting for 20.3% of the corpus, describes the normal functions of human genes and the health implications of genetic modifications. The Medical Tests category, making up 19.9% of the corpus, covers tests such as allergy skin tests, detailing their purposes, procedures, and possible results. The Health Topics category constitutes 18.8% of the corpus and serves as an encyclopedia covering body parts, therapies, and wellness issues, with content regularly reviewed and updated daily. The Introduction to Genetics category, comprising 9.75% of the dataset, provides fundamental explanations of human genetics concepts. Finally, the Dietary Supplements category, representing 5.15% of the dataset, offers descriptions of the effectiveness, usual dosages, and potential drug interactions of various supplements.\n1 https://medlineplus.gov/\nFigure 2: MedlinePlus Corpus Categories in tokens", "headings": ["3.1 Medical LLM Training"], "pages": [4], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/53", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 70.8565433, "t": 179.75601129885752, "r": 290.95183273586105, "b": 85.31082377558255, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 340]}, {"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 306.14430710000005, "t": 632.2076327488576, "r": 526.3308448104474, "b": 429.36302552558254, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [341, 1084]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/54", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "footnote", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 83.51017595, "t": 78.37423646355751, "r": 173.8054088859201, "b": 66.52178307051759, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 26]}]}, {"self_ref": "#/texts/55", "parent": {"cref": "#/pictures/1"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "caption", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 312.98764925000006, "t": 669.4889307205576, "r": 517.5798693495497, "b": 657.4833974739075, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 49]}]}], "headings": ["3.1 Medical LLM Training"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:20:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 20, "text": "The MedlinePlus training corpus is diverse and evenly distributed across various biomedical domains. For each category, one example is shown in Table 1. The examples highlight the diversity within the corpus which ensures a comprehensive representation of medical knowledge, which is crucial for training robust models capable of handling a wide range of medical and genetic information.", "contextualized_text": "3.1 Medical LLM Training\nThe MedlinePlus training corpus is diverse and evenly distributed across various biomedical domains. For each category, one example is shown in Table 1. The examples highlight the diversity within the corpus which ensures a comprehensive representation of medical knowledge, which is crucial for training robust models capable of handling a wide range of medical and genetic information.", "headings": ["3.1 Medical LLM Training"], "pages": [4], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/56", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 305.75128745000006, "t": 427.1183787988576, "r": 526.2366836605254, "b": 319.12251377558255, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 387]}]}], "headings": ["3.1 Medical LLM Training"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:21:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 21, "text": "To evaluate the performance of the trained medical LLMs as well as the baseline models, this work uses the prevalent multiple choice question answering benchmarks in the medical language model domain, including MMLU medical subjects (MMLU_MED), MedQA, and MedMCQA (Jin et al., 2021; Pal et al., 2022; Hendrycks et al., 2020). The evaluation metric utilized is classification accuracy based on logits. As all the benchmarks are in MCQ format, the token with the highest logit value can be selected as the model's predicted answer. The prompt used for evaluation as well as the example question and response are shown in Table 2. The models generate responses, and their accuracy is measured by comparing their responses to the expected correct answers.", "contextualized_text": "3.2 Medical LLM Evaluation\nTo evaluate the performance of the trained medical LLMs as well as the baseline models, this work uses the prevalent multiple choice question answering benchmarks in the medical language model domain, including MMLU medical subjects (MMLU_MED), MedQA, and MedMCQA (Jin et al., 2021; Pal et al., 2022; Hendrycks et al., 2020). The evaluation metric utilized is classification accuracy based on logits. As all the benchmarks are in MCQ format, the token with the highest logit value can be selected as the model's predicted answer. The prompt used for evaluation as well as the example question and response are shown in Table 2. The models generate responses, and their accuracy is measured by comparing their responses to the expected correct answers.", "headings": ["3.2 Medical LLM Evaluation"], "pages": [4], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/58", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 4, "bbox": {"l": 305.75128745000006, "t": 282.3701417488576, "r": 526.334772282811, "b": 65.97585707558244, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 751]}]}], "headings": ["3.2 Medical LLM Evaluation"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/chunks/rec_003258_acl.jsonl:22:{"candidate_id": "rec_003258_acl", "chunk_index": 22, "text": "10q26 deletion syndrome is a condition that results from the loss (deletion) of a small piece of chromosome 10 in each cell. ...", "contextualized_text": "3.2 Medical LLM Evaluation\nHealth Conditions\n10q26 deletion syndrome is a condition that results from the loss (deletion) of a small piece of chromosome 10 in each cell. ...", "headings": ["3.2 Medical LLM Evaluation", "Health Conditions"], "pages": [5], "meta": {"schema_name": "docling_core.transforms.chunker.DocMeta", "version": "1.0.0", "doc_items": [{"self_ref": "#/texts/61", "parent": {"cref": "#/body"}, "children": [], "content_layer": "body", "meta": null, "label": "text", "prov": [{"page_no": 5, "bbox": {"l": 79.95999845, "t": 757.2928866988575, "r": 279.2150721975746, "b": 717.0474090255826, "coord_origin": "BOTTOMLEFT"}, "charspan": [0, 128]}]}], "headings": ["3.2 Medical LLM Evaluation", "Health Conditions"], "captions": null, "origin": {"mimetype": "application/pdf", "binary_hash": 9293128959098327548, "filename": "rec_003258_acl.pdf", "uri": null}}}
